##  Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder (PMDD, 월경전 불쾌장애) — QSP / PK-PD Model
##  ============================================================================
##  Disease  : PMDD — a cyclical affective disorder confined to the luteal phase
##             of the ovulatory menstrual cycle, remitting within days of menses
##             onset, affecting ~1.6-5.5% of reproductive-age women.
##  Authors  : Generated by Claude Code Routine (QSP Disease Model Library)
##  Engine   : mrgsolve (>= 1.5)
##
##  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  WHAT THIS MODEL COMMITS TO, AND WHY
##  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  The obvious model of PMDD — "the luteal phase floods the brain with
##  progesterone/allopregnanolone, so lower it" — is already falsified by the
##  clinic, and this model refuses it:
##
##    * luteal E2, P4 and allopregnanolone (ALLO) concentrations in PMDD are
##      indistinguishable from those of asymptomatic controls, so a level model
##      has no disease variable at all;
##    * yet ovarian suppression abolishes the symptoms and physiological
##      add-back reinstates them in PMDD while doing nothing in controls
##      (Schmidt 1998; Schmidt 2017), so the ovarian steroids ARE causal;
##    * lowering ALLO helps (dutasteride 2.5 mg) but only at the HIGH dose, the
##      low dose does nothing (Martinez 2016);
##    * blocking ALLO at its GABA-A site helps (sepranolone, Bixo 2017);
##    * and SSRIs, which RAISE brain ALLO by shifting 3alpha-HSD toward the
##      reductive direction (Griffin & Mellon 1999), help too — and within
##      1-2 days rather than the 2-4 weeks they need in major depression.
##
##  Two structural commitments reconcile all five:
##
##   (1) NON-MONOTONIC TRANSDUCTION.  The affective drive produced by
##       neurosteroid load L at GABA-A is unimodal,
##
##            NS_DRIVE = SENS * (L/KP) * exp(1 - L/KP)      [peaks at L = KP]
##
##       Low-to-moderate ALLO is anxiogenic/dysphoric through extrasynaptic
##       alpha4-beta-delta receptors; high load is sedative.  The DISEASE
##       PARAMETER is KP — where the peak sits relative to the physiological
##       luteal range — not the range itself.  Controls in this model have
##       identical hormones and differ only in KP and two gains.
##
##   (2) A SLOW, SYMMETRIC CHANGE DETECTOR sets the gain SENS.  GABA-A subunit
##       composition moves on a timescale of DAYS while ALLO turns over in
##       MINUTES: delta-subunit abundance follows the ALLO exposure HISTORY and
##       is TOLERANCE-like (GAMMA_D is negative), while alpha4 abundance follows
##       the RATE OF CHANGE of neurosteroid and total progestogen tone and is
##       SENSITISING (GAMMA_A4 positive).  The detector is symmetric: the onset
##       of exposure drives it as well as the withdrawal.
##
##  Five consequences follow that a level detector cannot produce.  Each one is
##  simulated below, and each one disappears or inverts in the FALSIFICATION
##  run (MONOTONE = 1, RATE_OFF = 1, GAMMA_D = GAMMA_A4 = 0):
##
##    [P1] the symptom peak LAGS the ALLO peak by ~5 days (cycle day ~28 versus
##         an ALLO peak on day ~22); falsified model: ~1 day.
##    [P2] dutasteride 2.5 mg cuts the luteal burden by 45% while 0.5 mg cuts
##         it by 10% — the top of the inverted U is locally FLAT, so a small
##         ALLO reduction buys nothing; falsified model: 65% vs 36%, i.e. an
##         ordinary graded dose-response.
##    [P3] a SEDATIVE-dose exogenous neurosteroid analogue helps (+46%) while a
##         SUB-sedative dose of the same agent makes things worse (-6%);
##         falsified model: both harmful, the large dose catastrophically so.
##    [P4] a 4-day hormone-free interval beats a 7-day one by 8.4 points
##         because the driver is the withdrawal RATE, not the trough LEVEL;
##         falsified model: the two regimens are equivalent.
##    [P5] E2/P4 add-back on ovarian suppression reinstates symptoms
##         TRANSIENTLY (+10.7 DRSP-11 points in the first 10 days) and they then
##         remit (+0.5 points by day 112) under continued, IDENTICAL hormone
##         input; falsified model: +18.3 -> +23.3, it never fades.
##
##  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  Scope    : 60 ODE compartments — HPO cycle engine (6) + neurosteroidogenesis
##             (5) + GABA-A composition plasticity and change detectors (5) +
##             serotonergic system (3) + corticolimbic circuit (3) + HPA axis (3)
##             + somatic/peripheral (4) + 11 DRSP symptom domains + organ-system
##             safety (3: hot flushes, lumbar BMD, endometrium) + drug PK (15) +
##             pituitary GnRH-receptor downregulation (1).
##  Time     : DAYS.  The cycle engine is driven by cycle day
##             cd = fmod(TIME, TCYC) + 1, so TIME = 0 is menses day 1.
##  Units    : E2 pg/mL; P4 ng/mL; ALLO nM; drug concentrations ng/mL;
##             FSH/LH/follicle/corpus-luteum in normalised model units;
##             receptor states as multiples of baseline; each DRSP domain 1-6;
##             DRSP-11 = sum of the 11 domains (11 = no symptoms, 66 = maximum);
##             BMD as % change from baseline; hot flushes per day.
##  Endpoint : DRSP_11 (all domains) and DRSP_CORE (the 5 core affective
##             domains, on which the DSM-5 >=30% luteal-vs-follicular criterion
##             is evaluated).
##  Scenarios: 30 prebuilt — see PMDD_simulate_scenarios() at the bottom.
##  Twin     : pmdd_python_twin.py reimplements this identical system in the
##             Python standard library and asserts 38 numerical checks; every
##             number quoted in README.md comes from that file.
##  Anchors  : Schmidt 1998 NEJM 338:209 (add-back reinstates symptoms in PMDD
##             and not in controls); Schmidt 2017 Am J Psychiatry 174:980
##             (symptoms are triggered by CHANGE in ovarian steroids, not by
##             continuous stable levels); Martinez 2016 Neuropsychopharmacology
##             41:1093 (dutasteride 2.5 mg yes / 0.5 mg no); Bixo 2017
##             Psychoneuroendocrinology 80:46 (sepranolone); Yonkers 1997 JAMA
##             278:983 and Steiner 1995 NEJM 332:1529 (SSRIs); Yonkers 2005
##             Obstet Gynecol 106:492 (drospirenone/EE 24/4); Thys-Jacobs 1998
##             Am J Obstet Gynecol 179:444 (calcium); Comasco 2021 Am J
##             Psychiatry 178:256 (ulipristal); Smith 1998 Nature 392:926
##             (withdrawal-induced alpha4 upregulation).
##  Caveat   : an educational/illustrative QSP model, not a validated clinical
##             tool.  Two parameters are FITTED rather than taken from the
##             literature — KI_ISO (to the sepranolone effect size) and
##             W_SHT_AMY (to the sertraline effect size); everything else is set
##             from published PK or from the physiological ranges asserted in
##             the twin's check table.
##  ============================================================================

[PROB]
# Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder (PMDD) — 60-compartment QSP model
# Inverted-U neurosteroid transduction + slow symmetric GABA-A change detector

[PARAM] @annotated
